WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
We're not sure what's
causing it, but we do

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.620
know that it's located
on the edge of one of the

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.290
largest volcanic
complexes in the United

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.830
States, called the Long
Valley Caldera Complex.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.150
The Long Valley Caldera
is a 200 square mile

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.970
supervolcano that sits
under the Sierra Nevada

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:23.630
mountains. From the
skies all appears

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:27.200
peaceful. But beneath
these forested slopes lie

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:32.750
240mi³ of roiling 2000
degree Fahrenheit magma.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.050
Well, I think because of
where these tree patches

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.070
are situated.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.680
It may have something to
do with volcanic gases

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.840
that are emanating up
from the magma chamber

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:46.890
deep underground.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.020
Over 700,000 years ago.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.780
The Long Valley
supervolcano erupts with

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.780
2000 times the force of
that which destroyed

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:58.980
Mount Saint Helens.

